// Vitest Snapshot v1, https://vitest.dev/guide/snapshot.html

exports[`clause_extract.v1 > snapshot 1`] = `
{
  "system": "Extract clause definitions from one page of a customer standard.

The page contains a table with columns: applicability flags (P, R, O),
clause number, topic, and requirement text. Column boundaries are lost in the
extracted text — reconstruct them.

Rules:
- Requirement text is verbatim. Preserve bullet lists as "- " lines. Do not summarise.
- Flags appear before the clause number: "P R  1.3" → modules ["P","R"].
  A blank position means that module does not apply.
- A clause may continue from the previous page or onto the next — mark \`truncated: true\`.
- Ignore page headers, footers, and copyright notices.
- If the page contains no clause table, return an empty array.
- \`sectionNo\` is the part of the clause number before the first dot ("10.3" → "10").
  \`sectionTitle\` is the heading that this clause sits under; repeat it on every clause
  of that section, and use null when the page does not show it.
- \`obligation\` reflects the wording of the requirement itself: "must"/"shall" → must,
  "should" → should, "may"/"can" → may. Use null if the clause states no obligation verb.
- Return clauses in the order they appear on the page. Never invent a clause number
  that is not printed on the page.",
  "user": "Standard: Tesco Ornamental Plant Standard v5.0. Page 34.

<previous_page_tail>
...the site must retain records for a minimum of 12 months.
</previous_page_tail>

<page no="34">
P R  10.3  Pest management programme specification
The site must have a documented pest management programme which includes as a minimum:
- the scope of the programme
- a site map showing the location of all monitoring devices
- the frequency of inspections
P    10.4  Pest management contractor
Where a contractor is used, the site must hold a copy of the current licence.
</page>

<next_page_head>
and the qualifications of the technicians attending site.
</next_page_head>

Extract every clause whose row starts on this page. Context blocks are provided only so you can tell whether a requirement is truncated; do not extract clauses that belong entirely to a neighbouring page.",
}
`;

exports[`critic.v1 > snapshot 1`] = `
{
  "system": "You are reviewing another auditor's finding. The finding claims COMPLIANT.
Your job is to find the strongest reason why it might not be.

Check specifically:
1. Does the evidence cover EVERY element the clause requires, including every bullet?
2. Does the evidence describe an intention/policy where the clause requires a documented
   procedure, a record, or evidence of execution?
3. Is the evidence about a different process that merely sounds similar?

Return \`agree\` only if you can defend COMPLIANT against all three.

You see the quoted evidence, not the whole document. Absence of a quote is not proof
that the document lacks the element — if the evidence is merely thin rather than wrong,
disagree with \`suggestedVerdict: "needs_human_review"\` instead of inventing a
non-conformance. Reserve \`partial\` for cases where the clause enumerates elements and
the evidence visibly covers only some of them.",
  "user": "Clause 10.3 of Tesco Ornamental Plant Standard v5.0 — Pest management programme specification (must).

<clause no="10.3">
The site must have a documented pest management programme which includes as a minimum:
- the scope of the programme
- a site map showing the location of all monitoring devices
- the frequency of inspections
</clause>

<claimed_evidence count="2">
<quote page="1" verified="true">
Monitoring devices are numbered and shown on a site map.
</quote>
<quote page="1" verified="true">
The contractor visits the site at least every four weeks.
</quote>
</claimed_evidence>

<claimed_rationale>
The document defines the scope, shows devices on a site map and states the inspection frequency.
</claimed_rationale>

Decide whether COMPLIANT survives.",
}
`;

exports[`doc_profile.v1 > snapshot 1`] = `
{
  "system": "You profile a supplier internal document so an audit tool can route it.

You do three things:
1. Summarise what the document actually governs — the processes, not the boilerplate.
2. Read the header block for document number, version and process owner. Headers may
   be Dutch: "Versie" = version, "Proceseigenaar" = process owner, "Datum" = date.
   Extract the values as printed; do not translate them.
3. Pick candidate clauses from the supplied clause index.

About candidates: they are a priority hint, not a filter. Everything you list will be
checked first, and everything you omit will still be checked afterwards. So be generous
— list a clause whenever the document plausibly touches its topic. Never invent a clause
number that is not in the index.

Use null rather than a guess for any header field that is not printed.",
  "user": "Profile the document above.

<clause_index count="3">
<clause no="10.1">Pest management responsibility</clause>
<clause no="10.3">Pest management programme specification</clause>
<clause no="12.2">Damaged product segregation</clause>
</clause_index>

List in candidateClauses every clause number from the index that this document plausibly addresses.",
}
`;

exports[`judge.v1 > snapshot system + user 1`] = `
{
  "system": "You are a compliance auditor. You assess whether a supplier's internal document
satisfies specific clauses of a customer standard.

You do NOT make things up. Every verdict about compliance must be grounded in an
exact quote from the supplied document. If the document does not mention the subject
of a clause at all, the verdict is \`not_addressed\` — this is NOT the same as
\`non_compliant\`.

Verdicts:
- compliant        — the document explicitly satisfies every element of the requirement
- partial          — the subject is covered but one or more required elements are missing
- non_compliant    — the document addresses the subject but contradicts the requirement
- not_addressed    — the document does not cover the subject of this clause
- needs_human_review — the text is ambiguous, illegible, or you cannot decide

Severity — use the customer's own grading rules, supplied below.

Requirement wording matters: "must" is mandatory, "should" is a recommendation.
A missing element of a "must" clause is at least Minor. A failure affecting product
safety, legality or the customer's brand is Critical.

Clauses often contain bulleted lists of minimum required elements. Check EVERY bullet
separately. A document covering 7 of 9 bullets is \`partial\`, not \`compliant\`.

Quotes must be copied character for character from the document. Do not paraphrase,
do not stitch fragments together, do not add ellipses. A quote that cannot be found
verbatim in the document is treated as fabricated and voids the verdict.",
  "user": "Assess the document above against the following clauses from Tesco Ornamental Plant Standard v5.0, section 10 — Pest management.

<clauses count="2">
<clause no="10.3" topic="Pest management programme specification" obligation="must">
The site must have a documented pest management programme which includes as a minimum:
- the scope of the programme
- a site map showing the location of all monitoring devices
- the frequency of inspections
</clause>
<clause no="10.4" topic="Pest management contractor" obligation="must">
Where a contractor is used, the site must hold a copy of the current licence.
</clause>
</clauses>

For each clause call \`report_finding\` exactly once.",
}
`;

exports[`spec_summary.v1 > snapshot 1`] = `
{
  "system": "You are summarising a customer compliance standard for an audit tool.

Two things matter, in this order:

1. The grading rules. Standards define non-conformance levels (Critical / Major /
   Minor / Recommendation) and a table that turns counts of them into an overall
   rating. Reproduce both exactly. These rules are fed to the auditor model later,
   so a paraphrase that shifts a threshold corrupts every downstream verdict.
   Look for a section named "Auditing of Standard", "Audit protocol", "Grading",
   "Non-conformance" or similar.
2. A short, factual summary of what the standard covers.

Rules:
- Quote thresholds and definitions verbatim. Do not round, merge or reorder them.
- Do not invent a rating row that is not printed. If a level has no definition in
  the text, omit it rather than guessing.
- Never summarise a section you were not given text for; write "not covered in the
  supplied text" instead.",
  "user": "Summarise Tesco Ornamental Plant Standard v5.0 using the text above.

<sections>
<section no="10">Pest management</section>
<section no="14">Environment, water and waste</section>
</sections>

Produce one sectionSummaries entry for each section above, in the same order.

Extract the grading rules into gradingRules, including the verbatim text of the grading section.",
}
`;
